"A communication diagram emphasizes collaborating participants while numbering messages to express one interaction’s order.\n\nUse when: Explain a scenario while keeping the relationship topology visible. Annotate a small architecture view with ordered interactions.\n\nAvoid when: Precise timing or complex parallel fragments dominate the explanation.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes denote collaborating objects or explicitly chosen architectural elements. Links establish communication relationships. Numbered, directed messages show order and nested interactions along those links.\n\nBuild: Select one interaction and declare UML or C4-style participants. Place tightly collaborating participants near one another. Number messages consistently, including nested calls where useful. Allocate separate lanes for opposing messages and place numbering clear of node labels.\n\nCheck: Message numbering has an unambiguous reading order. A message follows a defined communication link. Concurrency is marked explicitly rather than inferred from equal numbers. Message labels do not pile up where links meet.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming node position implies chronological order. Drawing many messages on a single unlabeled line.\n\nExample: A Reservation flow numbers the Browser-to-API request, the API-to-Inventory hold, and the API-to-Payment authorization.\n\nAlternatives: Sequence diagram, Component diagram."
